Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ be the open unit disk and let $dA$ denote normalized area measure on $\mathbb{D}$. For $1<p<\infty$, the Bergman space $A^p(\mathbb{D})$ is the closed subspace of $L^p(\mathbb{D},dA)$ consisting of holomorphic functions. Let $P:L^2(\mathbb{D},dA)\to A^2(\mathbb{D})$ be the orthogonal Bergman projection (which extends boundedly to $L^p$ for $1<p<\infty$). For a measurable symbol $a:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$, the Toeplitz operator with symbol $a$ is defined on a dense subspace by
$$T_a f(z) = P(a f)(z), \qquad z\in\mathbb{D}.$$
A symbol $a$ is called radial if $a(z)=a(|z|)$ for a.e. $z\in\mathbb{D}$.

Develop a complete characterization, for each fixed $p\in(1,\infty)$ and in terms of the radial symbol $a$, of:
\begin{enumerate}
\item when $T_a$ extends to a bounded operator on $A^p(\mathbb{D})$;
\item when $T_a$ is compact on $A^p(\mathbb{D})$;
\item when $T_a$ is Fredholm on $A^p(\mathbb{D})$, together with a formula for its index and essential spectrum.
\end{enumerate}
That is, generalize to all Bergman spaces $A^p(\mathbb{D})$, $1<p<\infty$, the known boundedness, compactness, and Fredholmness results for radial-symbol Toeplitz operators on the Hilbert Bergman space $A^2(\mathbb{D})$.

